Third-party materials, stated exactly
-------------------------------------

Linux — https://kernel.org — GPL-2.0-only — (C) The Linux Kernel Authors

  Rust comments in crates/linux quote Linux C verbatim as the
  observable-semantics authority. Measured against the Linux 7.2.3 tree with
  scripts/ci/scan_linux_excerpts.py (whitespace-normalised lines of >= 40
  characters inside fenced code blocks, matched line for line; files counts
  source files holding such a block): mm 59 lines / 21 blocks / 9 files, net
  15 / 11 / 2, fd 13 / 3 / 3, mount 7 / 3 / 1, process 6 / 3 / 1, ipc 4 / 3 / 1,
  random 4 / 3 / 1, io-uring 1 / 1 / 1, aio 1 / 1 / 1; seccomp holds one further
  block whose lines are all shorter than 40 characters. 110 lines in total
  across 49 blocks in 20 files. At the inclusive >= 25-character threshold the
  same scope holds 211 lines in 59 blocks in 22 files. kernel/src carries a
  further 89 such lines in 43 blocks in 19 files.

  The same scan without the fence restriction adds 25 more verbatim lines in 5
  crates/linux files (4 crates) that sit in unfenced `///` prose lists and in
  `//` comments: io-uring 17 (registration.rs 16, sqe.rs 1), process 4
  (linux_abi.rs), fsnotify 3 (lib.rs), mm 1 (tests.rs); 41 such lines at the
  >= 25 threshold, 3 of which are Rust code rather than text (the
  MPOL_WEIGHTED_INTERLEAVE enumerator in mm/src/mempolicy.rs). 28 further
  >= 40 matches in bpf/src/uapi.rs and futex/src/lib.rs are ASCII separator
  rules (`-------`) that also occur in Linux headers and are not Linux text, so
  the scan filters them. crates/ax holds 7 further genuine Linux comment lines
  at >= 40, in tk-axfs-ng, tk-axfs-ng-vfs, and tk-axnet-ng comments, plus 8
  matches that are Rust code; of the 100 lines an --include-rules scan of that
  tree reports, 85 are smoltcp RFC bit-layout diagram rows, not Linux text.

  Attribution is by Linux file, and the precision varies: 18 of the 49 fenced
  crates/linux blocks carry an inline "Excerpt: Linux v7.2.3 <file>:<lines>"
  marker (21 marker lines in total: 12 in net, 5 in mount, 3 in random, 1 in
  aio; the three that open no >= 40 block are named in
  docs/upstream-provenance.md), 1 more has a matching Linux line-range cite in
  the surrounding prose, and the other 30 (every block in mm and fd, three in
  ipc, three in process) name the Linux file and, where Linux gives one, the
  enclosing function, with no line range. Nowhere else in the workspace does any
  comment carry that marker. The excerpts are documentation only: no Linux code
  is linked, assembled, or compiled into TheKernel, and the Apache-2.0 grant
  above does not extend to the
  quoted text. Every crates/linux package that holds Linux source text states
  this per package in its own NOTICE (aio, fd, fsnotify, io-uring, ipc, mm,
  mount, net, process, random, seccomp), and docs/upstream-provenance.md lists
  every citation, every gap, and the crates/ax lines, whose packages carry no
  NOTICE yet.

lwext4 — https://github.com/gkostka/lwext4 — vendored C, GPL-2.0-or-later

  crates/ax/tk-lwext4-rust/c/lwext4 vendors lwext4's C tree: 31 .c files
  (21,629 lines) plus 32 headers (7,258 lines), 28,887 lines between them
  (find c/lwext4 -name '*.c' -exec cat {} + | wc -l, and the same for '*.h',
  run from the crate root), self-declaring version 1.0.0
  (c/lwext4/Makefile:10-12). Per its own LICENSE and README, most modules are
  BSD-3-Clause, but src/ext4_xattr.c and src/ext4_extent.c carry
  "GPL version 2 or (at your option) any later version", which upstream states
  makes the whole library GPL-2.0-or-later. The crate declares license =
  "GPL-2.0", which in SPDX means GPL-2.0-only; the vendored files say
  v2-or-later, and only LICENSE.GPLv2 is shipped (no BSD-3-Clause text).

  This C is compiled and linked into every default kernel build:
  kernel/Cargo.toml requests the axfeat feature "fs-ng-ext4", which enables
  tk-axfs-ng's "ext4" feature, which pulls tk-lwext4-rust, whose build.rs runs
  `make musl-generic` over the vendored tree and links the result. A binary
  built that way is a combined work of Apache-2.0 and GPL-2.0-or-later code:
  the Apache-2.0 grant above is not a licence for that binary, and anyone
  redistributing it must also supply the complete corresponding source of the
  vendored C under its own terms. docs/licensing.md spells out those
  obligations. There is no non-default build recipe that omits this C:
  "fs-ng-ext4" is a literal entry in kernel/Cargo.toml's axfeat feature list,
  not a named tk-kernel feature, so no `cargo` or `./tools/thekernel.py` flag
  turns it off; docs/licensing.md section 2 states the one-line manifest change
  its owner would have to make first.

  crates/ax/tk-lwext4-rust/c/ext_images.7z is a binary test archive with no
  license statement of its own.

ArceOS, StarryOS, smoltcp, rust-fatfs, virtio-drivers, kernel-elf-parser

  crates/ax/* are forks, extractions, or vendored copies of these projects and
  keep their own license expressions and license texts in place — except as the
  lwext4 paragraph above states, and except that crates/ax/tk-axfs-ng-vfs
  declares "MIT OR Apache-2.0" while shipping only the Apache-2.0 text, as
  docs/licensing.md section 1 records. Upstream: ArceOS
  (https://github.com/arceos-org/arceos and the arceos-org split repositories,
  GPL-3.0-or-later OR Apache-2.0 OR MulanPSL-2.0), StarryOS
  (https://github.com/StarryOS/Starry, MIT OR Apache-2.0), smoltcp
  (https://github.com/smoltcp-rs/smoltcp, 0BSD), rust-fatfs
  (https://github.com/rafalh/rust-fatfs, MIT), virtio-drivers
  (https://github.com/rcore-os/virtio-drivers, MIT), and kernel-elf-parser
  (https://github.com/Azure-stars/kernel-elf-parser). docs/upstream-provenance
  .md records, crate by crate, the upstream project, its license, the local
  declaration, and the sync status; where this repository records no upstream
  revision it says "unknown" and names the evidence that would pin it rather
  than inventing a hash.
